anding_page_url": "https://works.hcommons.org:0nqg6-rea12", "pdf_url": null, "source": null, "license": null, "license_id": null, "version": "submittedVersion", "is_accepted": false, "is_published": false, "raw_source_name": null, "raw_type": "JournalArticle"}, "abstract": "Artificial intelligence is moving from systems that generate outputs to systems that can plan, select tools, initiate actions, coordinate workflows, and pursue goals with decreasing levels of direct supervision. This transition from generative AI to agentic AI changes the central philosophical and institutional question: the problem is no longer only whether a machine can produce a correct answer, but what happens to human agency, moral responsibility, and organizational accountability when execution is delegated to systems that can act. This paper introduces the Delegation Paradox: the more operational action is delegated to autonomous or semi-autonomous systems, the more human responsibility must be structurally concentrated around purpose, authority, boundaries, review, and consequence. Delegation can transfer work, but it cannot automatically transfer answerability. The paper distinguishes causal and operational agency from moral responsibility and examines the danger of an Answerability Gap, in which actions remain technically traceable while responsibility becomes socially and institutionally diffuse. It further develops the concept of Judgment Debt: the accumulated weakening of a person's or institution's capacity to evaluate, challenge, and justify decisions after repeated cognitive offloading to automated systems. Drawing on contemporary work on agentic AI governance, cognitive offloading, organizational redesign, automation, human oversight, and accountability, the paper proposes a Human Answerability Architecture: a six-layer framework for preserving human ownership of purpose, permissions, evidence, intervention, audit, and consequence. The central philosophical claim is that increasing machine autonomy does not eliminate human responsibility. It changes where responsibility must be located. The machine may act, but a human institution must still be able to answer. Author: Syed Raheel Shahzad Affiliation: The Syed Group ORCID: 0009-0001-7323-1577 ISNI: 0000 0005 3022 8433 Wikidata: Q139548931 Google Scholar: nRC4eGEAAAAJ The Syed Group Ltd ISNI: 0000 0005 3027 5408 Ringgold ID: 850493 Official Author Platform: SyedRaheelShahzad.com Institutional Platform: TheSyedGroup.com"}]", "excerpt_truncated": true, "source_sha256": "501eb1c84a8225321ab5c67e008711ef692a7a93cdbebfd92549c61f838ea3de", "verification_required": true, "topic_domain": "responsibility_formation", "evidence_role": "discovery", "host_tier": "discovery", "source_identity": null, "persistent_identifiers": ["doi:10.1007/s11948-018-0030-8", "doi:10.1007/s11948-018-0030-8.pdf", "openalex:W2792732529"], "source_candidates": ["https://doi.org/10.1007/s11948-018-0030-8", "https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/s11948-018-0030-8.pdf"], "source_candidate_identities": {}}
